\f4\fs26 The Travelator continued slowly, relentlessly clicking, wobbling and lurching along the warped steel rails. Kleo, Silo and Chilo were whispering among themselves, debating something of importance and nodding in agreement.\
\'93The timing is perfect.\'94 Chilo was nodding and even cracked a bit of a smirk as he glanced at Cracker and Sarah. \'93Two hundred and eighty three days exactly.\'94\
\'93From what I can tell she is the best candidate we have seen or are likely to see.\'94 Kleo whispered and then turned to his guests and began speaking, exposing dark stained teeth. \'93The Great One was not the first to cast light upon the malady of human imperfection, but he was the first to coherently challenge the structures of ignorance. Praise be to The Dawkins and the wisdom of his word.\'94 Kleo was quiet, serene and very serious.\
\'93Um, yay the Dawkins.\'94 Cracker was really hoping someone would clue him up about this Dawkins thing. He could not place the reference but, as always, was unable to admit ignorance.\
\'93Let him be praised\'94 Silo replied automatically.\
\'93Wisdom be The Dawkins.\'94 Chilo added, looking sideways at Kleo.\
\'93And the Dawkins be wisdom.\'94 Kleo was silent for the longest time before finally casting his gaze upon Sarah. \'93One of the most perplexing aspects of the human condition is our predisposition to believe in the promise of certainty, whenever it is peddled. We crave the comfort of knowing what will come to pass. Certainty, however, is illusory, it can only exist as a trick of the mind. It only draws breathe in an atmosphere of fantasy, in a place of shadows, far away from rational thought, where lies are laundered into comfortable truths and the impossible is plausibly accepted as fact.\'94 \
Kleo took a deep draught of air and saw Sarah sat upright on the chair, listening intently. Cracker hoped no-one would notice he was scratching his balls, or be polite enough to ignore it. Johnny was still out cold, but breathing steadily, quietly moaning at irregular intervals. \
\'93There are those who cynically hawk certainty in a myriad of forms. They seek to profit from human frailty and take advantage of our fears and prejudices. I am here to warn you against embracing the faith of certainty. Reason is our Saviour and doubt is our guide. 
\f5\i \'91Follow your reason as far as it will take you, without regard for any other consideration. Do not pretend that conclusions are certain when they are not demonstrated.\'92 So sayeth The Dawkins.\'94 
\f4\i0 \
\'93Praise be to him.\'94 Chilo was again tending to Johnny, checking his pulse and breathing.\
\'93Why do we crave certainty?\'94 Cracker was certain Kleo wanted him to ask that question. \
\'93I am glad you asked young Hardy. At the moment we were gifted sentience, we were overwhelmed by the realisation the world was a desperately dangerous place. Our reptilian brain stems caused us fright and panic at every minor peril, but gradually the mammalian and human upgrades were able to control fear by collecting and passing on knowledge. Over untold generations the collective wisdom became tradition, something to be followed without question.\'94 He took a deep breath. He had said this a few time before. \'93Wisdom, collective wisdom in the form of tribal lore, is only an assemblage of guesses as to the nature of things and falls well short of granting the certainty we crave. We are unable to ignore certainty when it is on offer, especially when it is served up as faith. When we embrace the faith of certainty, the truth is locked down tight.\'94\
\'93Even when smothered, the truth still exists.\'94 Sarah was not convinced.\
\'93Ah, the much vaunted power of the truth. It is a romantic notion to see the truth as an incontrovertible, immovable monolith that, when finally uncovered, shines magnificently, scorching ignorance away. The truth is fragile and delicate, it can never gift the certainty of a well-constructed falsehood and it wilts without support. Lawyers never enter court armed merely with the truth! They cannot possibly deflect every fib contrived by their adversaries. To obtain a favourable result, they fortify the truth with a false, but more palatable narrative and suppress valid evidence, highlighting improbable alternate scenarios to muddy the waters. Indeed, in an apparent search for the truth, many will do everything to avoid it.\'94 Chilo chipped in.\
\'93Who lies wins?\'94 Cracker thought he was doing well by keeping up, thus far.\
\'93The prevailing truth of the day is simply the opinion that has survived. Falsehood possesses an unending assortment of strategies and the real truth merely exists. When an inconvenient truth is exposed to the light of day, it is instantly subjected to attacks designed to undermine and weaken its power.\'94 Kleo gesticulated with upturned palms. \'93It is not always beautiful, indeed it can be ugly and distasteful. A pleasant fiction can be infinitely more desirable and if we cannot be certain of our security, we accept the illusion of it. The unreality created is held fast by blind faith that ignores absurdity and is hostile to challenge. So sayeth The Dawkins\'94\
\'93It does not seem to make sense. Why would anyone prefer to live in delusion?\'94 Sarah could not comprehend a civilisation where ignorance was a virtue.\
\'93Fallacious memes, religion being the most prominent example, have much positive reinforcement \'96 warm and comforting feelings of being loved and protected from a dangerous world. So say the Chronicles of The Dawkins. Praise be to him.\'94 Chilo cheerfully quoted. \
\'93There is a tendency for humans consciously to see what they wish to see. We literally have difficulty seeing negative connotations in our beliefs, while seeing all things that are positive. The diversity of irrational human beliefs, once entrenched in a culture, persist, evolve and diverge in a manner reminiscent of biological evolution. So sayeth the Dawkins.\'94 Kleo had reached a place of bliss, uplifted by the treasured words of his prophet. \'93What do you know of cult of religion?\'94\
\'93I studied it in Business Theology at School. I know that people used to worship gods and the people grouped themselves into different sects that had wildly differing views on how to worships these gods and were often in conflict with one another.\'94 Sarah said recalling distant memories from her early school days. \'93It all seemed a bit silly to me.\'94\
\'93I saw a few religious rallies broken up by the cops when I was a kid, but I can\'92t say that I took much notice. They were carrying around a giant lower case \'93t\'94, but I have no idea what they were squawking about.\'94 Cracker chuckled. \'93Man, the cops belted the absolute shit out them. I heard they were interrogated for weeks before their bodies were released.\'94 He continued to laugh. Religion was pretty funny.\
 \
\'93Human kind, in its infancy, could only fathom the inexplicable as being the work or the will of a divine being. It is a common phenomenon for young children to conjure up a friend unseen, a playmate and protector, someone that will always be there for them. Not unlike children, early man desperately wanted the solace of that personal guardian watching over them always, protecting them from spiders, snakes, lightening, floods, volcanos and diseases that would appear from nowhere. It is impossible to know, but it is most likely the first person to claim friendship with unseen spirits was one of those children who continued to carry undetectable guardians through to adulthood or, perhaps, the first to make contact with benevolence hidden were the insane, whose curious behaviours and incantations were mistaken for channelling the divine. However it happened, at some stage someone claimed to be in communication with God and others took him at his word.\'94\
 \
\'93An invisible defender-guy? Who would believe that?\'94 Upon thinking a little further, Cracker decided an invisible body guard on duty 24/7 might not be such a bad idea. \
\'93Everyone as it turned out, everyone. The work of the proto-god was not limited to protecting the tribe from lice infestation and those sub-human unbelievers from over the hill. His area of responsibility rapidly expanded to the punitive, demanding far more than a mere pound of flesh from believers and infidels alike. The rock-fall that killed half the tribe was a sign your invisible friend was unhappy with you, personally. How did you make him happy again? What did the disembodied and the eternal want? As mankind grew from infancy to childhood he experimented with many different models of placating his protector, most cults settled on an intense program of ritualism and sacrifice. A ludicrous and pointless endeavour.\'94\
\'93Human sacrifice?\'94 Sarah asked. \
\'93Sometimes yes, my word, yes. The adolescence of man saw humanity increasingly formalise rituals into compulsory unbending dogma, with rival sects clashing violently over who was had the correct set of beliefs and rituals.\'94\
\'93Men killed men because they didn\'92t grovel in the right way or served up the wrong dead things?\'94 Cracker just shook his head.\
\'93They did it to spread their particular variety of beliefs and rituals. Priests gathered money and garnered power, influencing Generals and Kings with promises of favourable consideration from above.\'94\
\'93You speak of paganism and idolatry. Isn\'92t it all ancient history?\'94 Sarah thought his overview of human history was more than a little sketchy.\
\'93I speak of ignorance wielding power and influence. I speak of unyoked delusion dressed as truth!\'94 Kleo was now animated, pacing up and down. \'93Living in an atmosphere of ignorance is very dangerous, particularly to those who challenge the status-quo, those who recognize the absurdity of unexamined faith. To insulate the meme from meaningful examination, citizens were controlled by filling their daily lives with the pointless busy-work of observance to sacrament. They were told what to do, how to do it and when.\'94\
\'93Everything, even how to fuck?\'94 Cracker was now not amused. That sort of intrusion of privacy was completely unacceptable, particularly if he was prevented from doing it doggy-fashioned.\
\'93Wow, is there anything else in there? Sarah inquired genuinely wanting to know if there was anything else on the mind of a man.\
\'93Yes. They were told who to marry and in what position to act out their predetermined love.\'94 Kleo did not like the tangent the conversation had slipped onto. \'93Religion demanded your faith and your faith devoured your life.\'94\
\'93Why would anyone submit to this without proof?\'94 Sarah attempted to keep the conversation above the waistline. \
\'93To quote the great one, \'91
\f5\i many religions teach the objectively implausible but subjectively appealing doctrine that our personalities survive our bodily death.
\f4\i0 \'92 All men must come to terms with the knowledge of their own mortality, but god provided the ignorant masses with a promise of eternal life in paradise. Some sects even had virgins thrown into the deal and in the absence of any other alternative, the post-mortem paradise plan seemed pretty good. Ridiculous egos of men readily accepted this insurance policy as fact. Rejecting the notion would expose a throbbing raw nerve - that there is nothing after death and we are, after all, unimportant. The thought of fading into non-existence was intolerable \'96 all knowledge and experience of a lifetime lost? Life after death was impossible to resist.\'94\
\'93Virgins? A pretty rare commodity.\'94 Cracker was looking directly at the only virgin in a 10 kilometre radius, but Sarah, who could feel his mocking gaze, did not give him the satisfaction of glancing his way. \'93Do you reckon they could chuck in a couple of professionals? You know, so you can get a good workout.\'94\
\'93The Dawkins tells us the life after death doctrine reduces the wonder of human existence, the delight and improbability of being alive, to a test. Whether we have been good enough to get into paradise. The invisible friend as protector was gone, having morphed into an all seeing all knowing accountant, carefully scribing every transgression in a heavenly black box recorder.\'94 Kleo seemed to be saddened a little. \'93The God-being deliberately made us flawed and then set about punishing us for it.\'94\
\'93Piety for life? No-one could pass that test.\'94 Cracker knew he would have no hope. Some of the shenanigans he had got up to over the years would make Satan shake his head in disbelief.\
\'93One sect did provide sinners with a loop-hole by allowing into their heaven, anyone who accepts Jesus as lord and saviour, following his famous suicide mission in the middle-east.\'94 \
 \'93How did we get rid of them?\'94 Cracker was actually interested now, he guessed it was pretty violent.\
 \'93Science began to make inroads here and there, but god-followers fought back at every turn, forcing greater minds to recant and abandon legitimate science and drop on bended knee to kiss a priest\'92s ring. Scientific advancement can only be slowed, not halted and as the centuries passed, snippets were gathered here and there and built upon until Darwin, the One before the One, realised the glimmering beauty of evolution and natural selection. Science and reason gave man the chance to release himself from a fantasy world of angels, demons, heaven and hell, but many refused to see the light, remaining devout and even militant.\'94\
\'93I see a war coming.\'94 Cracker liked war-vids and had an unfortunate romantic view of human conflict, erroneously figuring that he, just like the central protagonist in all the best movies, would also survive until the credits rolled. He would stroll into the sunset, with a huge machine gun slung across his sweaty, blood splattered back, on the arm of an impossibly attractive blonde soldier-babe who, for reasons unknown, chose to do battle in a bikini top and short-shorts.\
\'93Killing others in the name of God became increasingly popular with the devout, in fact the more devout one became, the more reasons existed to puncture, shoot and blow up the opposition. Indifferent as it is, however, evolution had other plans and fashioned a golden strand of DNA of an infinitely superior kind, The Dawkins. The Dawkins constructed his elegant defence of evolution, what we now call Rational Evolutionism, giving rational thought room to take root and grow.\'94\
\'93Can\'92t religion and evolution co-exist?\'94 Sarah questioned.\
\'93No. Science demands proof, actual evidence that can stand up to scrutiny. Religion demands belief without proof and sometimes belief notwithstanding proof to the contrary. As the Dawkins correctly asks, \'91W
\f5\i hy should science respectfully tip-toe away
\f4\i0 ?\'92\'94 Kleo waited for an answer but none came. \'93If a religious man accepts evolutionary science, then he must accept the notion that God created the universe only to wait 14 billion years for the advent of human worship. It seems an awfully long time to wait for a bit of adoration.\'94 \
\'93Seems a little self-absorbed too. I am very glad this nonsense has been eradicated.\'94 Sarah felt glad that science had inoculated her against such backward thinking. \
\'93Not eradicated, still here but in a different form. In the Primary Trade Dominion we worship money and make no mistake it is a religion. We are conditioned by fear to worship at the altar of profit, with the market cast as vengeful god. We are drones, trained by rote to follow fiscal dogma without question.\'94 Kleo fidgeted as he spoke, ill at ease with the knowledge that the market, unlike most other gods, was easily manipulated. \'93We televise human sacrifice to sell soap powder. We contrived a system that ensures many live in poverty and then seek to punish them for being poor. We are no different and we are no better than our ignorant forefathers. Portable communicators, home delivery pizza and interactive holo-vids do not make us anymore enlightened.\'94\
\'93How did the PTD dump religion?\'94 Cracker was intrigued.\
\'93Decades ago, an accident of history allowed a demagogue, Dunning Kruger, to seize the Presidency of the Union of Capitalist Atlantic States, the most powerful nation on earth at the time. He was a buffoon with no shame and possessed of a cynical, chameleonic ability to shape-shift into anything the moment demanded. He had no regard for truth and little regard for his fellow man, even the rich and powerful were unceremoniously cast aside when they dared challenge him. He lied and then lied about lying and then lied about that. Unable to work from a common set of facts, observers and pundits were so distracted, so obsessed with proving the President a liar that none thought to look through the web of disinformation to devine his actual agenda. He had created his own reality and in this alternative truth environment he convinced many that he and only he had the answers.\'94\
\'93Lying is the cornerstone of politics, is it not?\'94 Sarah\
\'93Politicians are very miserly with the truth, that is a fact, but Kruger made falsehood an art form, deliberately confusing issues to promote the notion that he was the only one who could navigate to safe waters. No matter what else, he sold certainty and being unable to resist the promise of certainty voters flocked to his cause. He promised voters the world and delivered nothing. He promised fair dealing with the international community, but delivered hostility and ultimatums. He strong armed and invaded oil rich nations to commandeer their resources and killed thousands just so Atlantians could fill up on cheaper fuel. When he was gifted an opportunity to deploy nuclear weapons, as he had threatened many times, he launched without hesitation.\'94\
\'93He nuked the oil fields?\'94 Cracker was gob-smacked.\
\'93No, he wasn\'92t that stupid. He vaporised two million of their religious brothers who had invaded this very country.\'94 Kleo was clearly distressed by the demise of the old country.\
\'93The Dominion?\'94 Cracker had not been a particularly attentive student at school and was missing vast swathes of commonly known facts.\
\'93No, silly, Ulyssia.\'94 Sarah interrupted. \'93This whole continent used to be one country.\'94\
\'93Most criticism of his polices had come from middle-eastern countries, but were quickly silenced with threats no longer empty. He continued his policy of ultimatum diplomacy, deploying the nukes whenever he found disagreement.\'94\
\'93Didn\'92t anyone retaliate?\'94 Cracker figured someone would have.\
\'93Yes, of course, but the defence net in place over the country managed to bring down all of the missiles, with the mysterious exception of missiles in-bound for cities that did not vote Kruger. These places, mostly on the east and west coast, were devastated.\'94\
\'93How does this have anything to do with The Dawkins?\'94 Sarah thought this was getting way off topic.\
\'93The Dawkins despised ignorance in all forms and Kruger was idiocy personified, but his moronic policy of random, but regular, nuking of wrong thinking people, wiped out a couple of religions entirely and many others came to the conclusion that if Dunning Kruger can be the most powerful person on earth, there is no god. God could be forgiven for bone cancer in infants or for allowing the holocaust, but this was a bridge too far. The hitherto largely unread Chronicles of The Dawkins began a resurgence, enunciating the ultimate truth, that there is no ultimate truth. Praise be to him.\'94 \
\'93In the newly formed Primary Trade Dominion, it was obligatory for any organisation, religions included, to contribute to economic activity and behave as responsible corporate citizens, complying with all tax, corporate and consumer legislation. It was the latter that brought them to their knees. Countless prayers had remained unanswered and consumers sued their local god franchises in their millions, all crying fraud as the God they had invested in so heavily was not producing dividends. Religion was required to present proof that God did live up to the hype, but no evidence could be produced. The payouts were so enormous, that the sects were bankrupted or insurance premiums became so exorbitant that they had to shut down operations. Religious gatherings became unjustifiably expensive due to public liability insurance premiums.\'94 \
\'93In the absence of religion, why didn\'92t science and reason take root?\'94 To Sarah it seemed natural that science and reason would have filled the vacuum.\
\'93Do you not attend Sunday morning financial statements at your local Chamber of Commerce?\'94\
\'93Yes, but it would be noticed if I didn\'92t\'94\
\'93When religion was in it\'92s final death throws, the viral cleansing hit Ulyssia and wiped out people by the millions. Some jumped back on the religious bandwagon, citing the plague as a sign from an angry god, but those were quickly shouted down. Opportunists used the viral cleansing as a chance to promote their own private agendas. As the country of Ulyssia broke up into the Primary Trade Dominion and the radio-active Western Trade Dominion, rabid anti-Atlantian sentiment grew into a nationalist policy of isolationism. It was decided to base the economy on high-tech weapons development and manufacture. To ensure the success of the new order, icons, rituals and symbols of religion were recycled into the ideology under which we currently suffer. We do not yet live in the age of reason, but the age of shameless lies and unfettered profiteering. The age of reason will begin when Dawkinsian Evolutionism is fully embraced.\'94 Kleo looked spent. \
 \
\'93But our society is the very definition of survival of the fittest. Does that not fit snugly into Evolution?\'94 Sarah liked stirring the pot a bit, even if she was a big fan of Darwin.\
\'93The Dawkins was very explicit about this, his name be praised.\'94 Silo faced glowed when he said the name of the great one. \'93It is written in his chronicles - survival of the fittest is a good way for nature to select species but a bad way for men to run a civilisation. His words be wise and wise be his words.\'94 \
\'93Praise be to the Dawkins,\'94 both Chilo and Kleo chimed in with perfect timing.\
 \'93The Chronicles of The Dawkins unmistakably document his strong conviction that the individual should be given the greatest opportunity to develop, evolve if you will, without being chained down by dogma. Can you imagine all the brilliant minds that have gone to waste? Young minds should be allowed to grow without being infected with viral memes. That cannot occur in our society of yawning inequality. Evolution will not let it stand. Another will take the place of the Dawkins, the one-after-the-one.\'94 \
\'93The one-after-the-one.\'94 Silo and Chilo in perfect unison.\
\'93The Messiah? Give me a fucking break.\'94 Cracker was greatly amused, these guys really believed their own bullshit.\
\'93No, not a messiah, the next great intellect. The great leader to come. The nature of genetics insists that evolution will, eventually, throw up the precise DNA required. It is an operation in probability not prophesy. The Dawkins would forgive your ignorance.\'94 Kleo was calm and remained unflustered in the face of Cracker\'92s disrespect. \
	\
\'93Cometh the hour cometh the man?\'94 Sarah was not too sure of his understanding of probability. \'93This can only be conjecture, how can anyone know what will come to pass?\'94\
\'93Prophecy, messianic or otherwise, is always self-fulfilling. We retrofit the personality to neatly dovetail with revelation.\'94 \
\'93Is that your Plan? Find someone to retrofit?\'94 As with his ignorance, Cracker\'92s cynicism was always close to the surface and exerted itself often enough to be a defining characteristic. \
\'93The identity of the one-after-the-one will be undeniable.\'94 Kleo knew this, almost certainly, to be true.\
\'93The one-after-the-one. Praise be to him.\'94 Silo and Chilo incanted perfectly synchronised.\
A loud bang from ceiling of the car startled the group, all suddenly glancing above attempting to discern the origin. Several more hits overhead saw dust and splintered wood shower down, covering Cracker and Sarah with debris and fine powder.\
\'93Jesus Christ!\'94 As Cracker had said thousands times before, but on this occasion he was finally aware he had, for much of his life, been invoking the name of some ancient deity. Blinded
\f5\i  
\f4\i0 by clumps of dust and fine splinters he was in some obvious distress, raising his arms to the horizontal and swinging them wildly, back and forth, desperately trying to locate something or someone to grab. Failing miserably, he dropped to his knees and rubbed his eyes vigorously with the heel of his hands and the back of his forefingers, but only managing to scrape the fragments across his corneas, increasing discomfort. Sarah too, was struggling with smarting eyes, but had the presence of mind not to scratch or rub and was sufficiently oriented to relocate her seat, throwing the remainder of her wine into her eyes. It stung like crazy, exactly as she had expected, but most of the offending material was flushed free granting her blurred vision.\
\'93Intruders!\'94 Chilo yelled and brought Sarah to the ground. \'93Stay here young miss. We will repel these unbelievers.\'94 That was okay with her, she needed time to regain her sight, but as the attack from above continued, the car filled with fine particulate matter, cancelling out any gains in vision. Judging by the ruckus, their three hosts were doing fine against the unknown intruders, Kleo yelling instructions triggering his apprentices into immediate and decisive action, shoring up vulnerable spots on the skin of their fortress. \
\'93Hole in the roof!\'94 Chilo barked. \'93Smoke grenade.\'94\
\'93Fight for The Dawkins my apprentices!\'94 Kleo rallied as the combat intensified. \'93Be gone foul interlopers!\'94 He wondered if he was over-doing it.\
Sarah was reasonably able to see now and stood to her feet to find she was only able to discern shadows slipping through the dust and smoke suspended in the stifling air. Without warning she was knocked backwards with a sharp blow to her abdomen, nothing to make her scream, but certainly painful enough for her to roll into the foetal position rocking for a moment or two as the discomfort dissipated. She bounced to her feet to join in the fight, but the din had been replaced with silence.\
\'93We are out of The Pale. We are safe now.\'94 Kleo opened the main entrance door allowing the dust and smoke to escape and visibility to return to the car, revealing a jagged fissure in the roof easily broad enough for a man to drop through. \'93Is anyone injured?\'94\
\'93I can\'92t see, but apart from that I\'92m okay.\'94 Cracker was still rabidly rubbing his eye sockets.\
\'93I\'92m fine.\'94 Sarah picked up a chair and sat down slowly. Looking about, she saw Silo and Chilo were already busily waving drills, patching the hole, fixing down rusty misshapen bits of scavenged sheet metal with massive screws that bit deeply, penetrating the roof. The ceiling was scattered with similar patches leaving the roof outside, Sarah imagined, resembling a porcupine with a crew cut. \'93What just happened?\'94\
\'93Gazumpers.\'94 Kleo panted as he scratched his beard. \
\'93Where did they go?\'94 Sarah felt the urge to deal out some justice, but she was left with no candidates.\
\'93They slipped back out into The Pale.\'94 Silo counselled calmly. \'93They are gone.\'94\
\'93They are not to be reviled, Sarah. They have been denied the wisdom of The Dawkins and their personal evolution has been stifled by lies and greed. The word of the Dawkins will change all of this.\'94 Kleo sat down and gently took Sarah\'92s hand.\
\'93They are thieves!\'94\
\'93Yes, they are, but perhaps not forever. The one after the one will come.\'94\
\'93His words will be known.\'94 Silo and Chilo were well practiced.\
\'93How is Johnny?\'94 Sarah suddenly remembered her companion and glanced fleetingly at the crumpled suit on the floor.\
\'93Sleeping beauty can wait. I can\'92t fucking see!\'94 Cracker was in no mood. \'93I need a bit of a hand over here thanks.\'94 \
Sarah grabbed the jug of wine and stomped over to Cracker and stood before him, feet shoulder width apart and one hand on her hip. She smiled and motioned to empty the contents on his head, but baulked and looked over to Kleo, \'93Don\'92t suppose you have some water?\'94\
\'93Yes. Please don\'92t waste anymore.\'94 She smiled, returned to the table and poured herself a drink.\
Silo was up and already handing Cracker a cup of water which was maniacally hoisted directly into his eye socket, cracking his skull. Without a word he waved the cup about which, when replenished, was unceremoniously slammed into his other eye. \'93Oh, thanks. That is so much better.\'94\
\'93You okay now?\'94 Sarah return to Johnny who appeared to be waking up. \'93Don\'92t worry, we will take care of you.\'94\
\'93Well my friends, we will arrive at your destination shortly.\'94 Kleo smiled. \'93Gather up your friend. We will meet again, I know this to be true. Praise be to The Dawkins.\'94\
\'93Is there enough time to tell us of Jamison Hardy?\'94 Cracker was interested in his new relative.\
\'93I can only tell you of the days leading up to the Great Viral Cleansing. It is rumoured that one of his research facilities was quarantined suddenly and sealed off permanently under circumstances that were never really clear. It was an underground facility that produced some of the nastiest microbes ever cooked up. Mr Hardy had no interest in bio-weaponry and was in the process of selling off the assets when he suddenly shut it down. No explanations, not to his closest confidants, not even to me.\'94 Being left out of the loop by the great Jamison Hardy had sliced Kleo to the core.\
\'93The Viral Cleansing.\'94 She figured this was where he was going. \'93Something got out didn\'92t it?\'94\
\'93That is a mystery even to me, Sarah. A mystery to solve.\'94 Kleo replied. \
\'93Can I ask an obvious question? If religion is the enemy, why are you and your disciples dressed like Druids and delivering sermons? Is this not a religion?\'94 Sarah finally pointed at the elephant in the corner. \
\'93Ritual and ceremony are not necessarily religious in nature and people seem to derive comfort from customarily repeated practices. If you want to bring the concept of god into our belief system, we are talking of the god of the great prophet, Karl Sagan. Quoted by The Dawkins himself, 
\f5\i If by \'93God\'94 one means the set of physical laws that govern the universe, then clearly there is such a god.\'94 
\f4\i0 Looking at Sarah, Kleo smiled brightly. \'93We will use the comfort of ritual to spread our word and you will be a part of it. You will bear witness to it all, Sarah.\'94 \
Praise be to the Dawkins.\
